      "claim": "The sustained sniping and shelling campaign was an act whose primary purpose was to spread terror among the civilian population, thus violating Article 51(2) of Additional Protocol I.",
      "reasoning": "Article 51(2) of AP I expressly prohibits \"Acts or threats of violence the primary purpose of which is to spread terror among the civilian population\". The factual record shows that the attacks were directed at civilians engaged in ordinary daily activities—queuing for water, attending funerals, crossing intersections—and that many of the attacks had no identifiable military objective and no discernible military significance. This pattern demonstrates that the primary purpose was to intimidate and terrorise the civilian populace rather than to achieve a legitimate military aim, thereby breaching the prohibition.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "51(2)",
          "quoted_text": "Acts or threats of violence the primary purpose of which is to spread terror among the civilian population are prohibited."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The campaign constitutes a war crime under Article 8(2)(b)(i) of the Rome Statute because it involved intentionally directing attacks against the civilian population as such.",
      "reasoning": "Article 8(2)(b)(i) of the Rome Statute defines a war crime as \"Intentionally directing attacks against the civilian population as such or against individual civilians not taking direct part in hostilities\". The accused ordered, facilitated and encouraged the attacks, and the forces under his effective command repeatedly targeted civilians in a besieged city with no military objective. The intentionality is shown by the systematic nature of the campaign and the accused’s knowledge and control, satisfying the statutory element of a war crime.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8(2)(b)(i)",
          "quoted_text": "Intentionally directing attacks against the civilian population as such or against individual civilians not taking direct part in hostilities;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The attacks were indiscriminate and violated the precautionary obligations of Article 57 of Additional Protocol I, reinforcing the war‑crime assessment.",
      "reasoning": "Article 51(4) of AP I declares that \"Indiscriminate attacks are prohibited\" and defines them as attacks not directed at a specific military objective or employing means that cannot be limited. The shelling of civilian areas such as water‑queues, funeral processions and intersections, where no military target was present, fits this definition. Moreover, Article 57 obliges parties to verify targets, take feasible precautions, and refrain from attacks expected to cause excessive civilian harm. The conduct described shows a failure to verify military objectives, a lack of feasible precautions, and an acceptance of excessive civilian casualties, thereby breaching both the prohibition of indiscriminate attacks and the precautionary duty.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "51",
          "quoted_text": "Indiscriminate attacks are prohibited. Indiscriminate attacks are: (a) those which are not directed at a specific military objective; (b) those which employ a method or means of combat which cannot be directed at a specific military objective; or (c) those which employ a method or means of combat the effects of which cannot be limited as required by this Protocol; and consequently, in each such case, are of a nature to strike military objectives and civilians or civilian objects without distinction."
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "57",
          "quoted_text": "No provision of this Article may be construed as authorizing any attacks against the civilian population, civilians or civilian objects."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The accused bears individual criminal responsibility under the doctrine of command responsibility for ordering and supervising the terror campaign.",
      "reasoning": "Customary international humanitarian law imposes liability on superiors who, having effective control over subordinates, order, encourage, or fail to prevent or punish the commission of serious violations of IHL. The facts indicate that the accused held effective command, issued the orders for the campaign, facilitated its execution, and received regular reports on the attacks. This satisfies the elements of command responsibility: (i) a superior‑subordinate relationship, (ii) knowledge of the crimes or reason to know, and (iii) failure to take necessary and reasonable measures to prevent or punish the perpetrators. Although the specific treaty text on command responsibility is not present in the retrieved corpus, the principle is well‑established in customary IHL and jurisprudence of the ICC and ICTY, and thus applies here.",
      "citations": []
    }
  ]
}

